# The booth demo

Ten minutes including questions, for the second judge team (plan Section 15; the format is in `docs/competition/grand-final-2026.md`). Two people: a **narrator** who never touches the keyboard and an **operator** who never speaks over the narrator. Every step below is also driven by a check, named beside it, so the path the judges see is the path the checks prove.

## Before each run

| Do | Why | Checked by |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Build once: `npm run build`, then serve it (`npm run preview`, or the local API with `npm run dev` if the live fetch is wanted) | The demo runs the built app | `npm run smoke` runs the same build |
| Test the network. If it is down, the dated saved sample stands in, and the narrator says so before Watch | Honest provenance | The tour's `sampleIfFailed` step; the curriculum smoke |
| Sign in as Dr Sobri; Guide & progress, Backups: restore the starting backup if a previous run left records | One dataset for everything | `npm run smoke:curriculum` restores a backup the same way |
| Claude Desktop configured with the door (`docs/doors.md`), the app's export saved where the config points | The door answers from what is on screen | `node --test src/prove-mcp.ts`; the manual run in `docs/doors.md` |
| On the laptop and a USB stick: the narrated 60-second recording, the 90-second film (`video/out/loop90.mp4`), the full tour recording | Never debug live | Played once on the laptop before the session |

## The path, six minutes

| Step | The operator does | The narrator says | Do not say | Checked by |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| Course | Opens CS310 and sets the term, 2026/2027 semester 1 | "One course, one term, the whole loop. Every record we make carries this term." | That CS310 is a university's real course: it is the demo's seeded course | The tour and `npm run smoke` |
| Watch | Fetches Stripe's public board on Greenhouse (or the saved sample), keeps the postings | "Research the lecturer runs, on request, from a board whose policy we state. Each posting keeps its link, its date and a fingerprint." | "We scrape LinkedIn", or anything about boards we do not read | `npm run smoke`; `node --test src/prove-collect.ts` |
| Report | Names the skills, scans, confirms the proposals beside their sources; types a paraphrase and lets it fail; reviews the postings; shows the count; maps dashboards to CLO5; prepares and sends the brief | "A quotation is exact or it is refused. The count is over the postings the lecturer reviewed, with its interval." Pause after the refusal. | A percentage without its denominator; "employers want", where the screen says "postings quote" | `npm run smoke`; `node --test src/prove-evidence.ts src/prove-research.ts` |
| Decide | Switches to Dr Rahman; opens the brief on Home; writes the reason; records adopt | "People decide, and the decision leaves a record: who, when, why, on which brief, for which version and term." | That the decision is the university's approval: formal approval stays in its process | `npm run smoke` asserts the decision and the event order |
| Update | Back to Dr Sobri; starts the revision, sharpens the outcome, adds the dashboards topic within the hours, checks, adopts; opens History | "The change the decision approved, checked against the hours and the protected topics. The old version is kept." | That the revision is better teaching: it is a checked change | `npm run smoke`; `node --test src/prove-curriculum.ts` |
| Teach | Saves the week 6 session and downloads its pack; opens it in PowerPoint | "An editable pack built from the records. No model wrote a word of it." | That the pack is ready to teach without the lecturer's review | `npm run smoke` asserts both downloads; `node --test src/prove-slides.ts` |
| Learn | Files the sample results under the term and the version taught | "The app's sample results, synthetic marks, matched to the version taught, with n of N. We claim no learning gain until the benchmark has run." | That the marks are a real cohort's; any learning gain, any prediction, anything from the mock cohort | `npm run smoke`; `node --test src/prove-journey.ts` |
| The door | In Claude Desktop, asks for CS310's evidence report | "The university's own assistant asks Lyceum for the evidence. Every line comes back with its receipt." | That remote access, ChatGPT, Copilot or the LMS doors are built: they are planned | `node --test src/prove-mcp.ts`; `node mcp/try.ts` |

Home then shows the ring closed for the term; the narrator ends on "Every semester adds a ring."

## If something fails

Say what happened in one sentence, switch, and keep going: the saved sample if the network fails (the log says so on screen); the 60-second recording if the app fails; the 90-second film if the laptop's screen fails us; the full tour recording if the questions run long. Each switch is rehearsed as a step of its own, with its sentence.

## Questions

The bank is plan Section 14's ten questions. Two answers are rehearsed word for word and are in the deck's notes for slide 11 (`deck/index.html`); the Story lane writes the other eight in the team's words, one breath each, ending on what is on screen. What each answer rests on:

| Question | The answer rests on | On screen |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Is this scraping? | Collection runs only when the lecturer asks, from a named employer's public board through the API its owner documents (Greenhouse or Lever), with the source policy shown; each posting keeps its link, its date and a fingerprint (`src/lib/collect.ts`) | Watch: the source policy and the run log |
| Where is the AI? | Rehearsed word for word (slide 11's notes) | The door in Claude Desktop |
| Why not ChatGPT alone? | Rehearsed word for word (slide 11's notes) | The refused paraphrase |
| How do we know the postings are representative? | We do not claim they are: counts are over the postings the lecturer reviewed, from a named board, with Wilson intervals | Count: n of N and the interval |
| What breaks first at scale? | The review time, because a person reviews every posting a count uses; and the workspace lives in one browser until the shared workspace is built (planned) | Slide 8: what is not built |
| Does it decide the syllabus? | No: a person records the decision and its reason at Decide, and formal approval stays in the university's process | The decision record |
| Why another tool? | It reaches the tools lecturers already use: Claude reads the evidence report through the local door, and the pack is an editable PowerPoint; the LMS and office doors are planned | Slide 9: the doors |
| What does the free tier cost us? | Slide 10's notes: "Nothing to try: one course, one laptop, no account." | Slide 10: the ladder |
| What is the benchmark? | Predictions frozen before a semester and scored against the last cohort carried forward (plan Section 9); it has not been run, so no learning gain is claimed | Evaluation |
| Is lime readable? | Lime text sits only on forest, where it passes WCAG AAA, and never carries text, bars or lines on a light surface (`docs/design/colour-scheme-audit-2026-10-22.md`) | The app's header, the poster |

Every number the narrator may say is in `docs/claims.md`.

## Rehearsal

Twice on 26 October, with `npm run smoke` under turbo as the warm-up, timed: the path under six minutes, the whole slot under ten.

===== FILE: JUDGES.md =====
# Five minutes with Lyceum (for judges)

Lyceum turns real job postings into a course decision with receipts, every semester, inside the tools lecturers already use. What is worth judging is the loop: six steps a lecturer runs on one course for one term, each ending in a record, with people deciding at the one step that changes the course. No model key or external account is needed. Live collection needs the local API and a network connection; a dated saved sample of real postings works without them.

```bash
npm install
npm run dev         # the web workspace and the local API (document parsing, live board collection); open the printed URL
```

`npm run build` then `npm run preview` serves the workspace alone; the saved sample still works there. On Windows PowerShell, use `npm.cmd` if the execution policy blocks `npm.ps1`.

## The loop

| Step | The question it answers | The record it leaves |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Course (setup) | What does this course promise today, and for which term? | The outline, and the term every later record carries |
| Watch | What are employers asking for, right now? | Postings fetched on request, each with its link, its date and a fingerprint |
| Report | What exactly do they ask, how often, and what does the course do about it? | Exact quotations, the reviewed denominator, counts with intervals, a mapping, the brief |
| Decide | What will we do, and who said so? | A decision record: adopt, retain, defer or return, by whom, when, why, on which brief, version and term |
| Update | What does the approved change look like, within our hours? | A checked revision, adopted as a new version with the old one kept |
| Teach | What will the class do, and with what? | A session linked to an outcome and its editable teaching pack |
| Learn | Did it work, as far as we can honestly tell? | The term's results, matched to the version taught, with n of N |

The ring on Home closes when every step has its record for the term.

## Option A: hands off

On the public landing page (`#/index`, **See how Lyceum works** on the sign-in screen), choose **Watch the tour**, about 7 minutes. A cursor plays over the real app: Dr Sobri sets the term, fetches Stripe's public board (the dated saved sample stands in if the network fails, and the log says so), confirms exact quotations, watches a paraphrase be refused, reviews the postings, counts, maps dashboards to CLO5 and sends the brief; Dr Rahman decides adopt with a reason; Dr Sobri adopts the revision, saves the session and its pack, files the sample results, and Home shows the ring closed. Press Escape to take over; playing resets the local workspace and warns first if it finds saved work.

## Option B: hands on

Sign in as **Dr Sobri**, open **Courses**, then **CS310**. The rail beside the course follows the loop; each step opens with its question and ends with the handoff to the next.

1. **Course.** Set the term, 2026/2027 semester 1.
2. **Watch.** Keep the saved sample, fetch, select the postings and keep them.
3. **Report.** In **Quote**, name `SQL, Python, dashboards|dashboard, stakeholders|stakeholder`, scan, and confirm the proposals beside their sources; then type a paraphrase under **Quote a requirement by hand** and watch it be refused. In **Review**, acknowledge the extraction and mark the postings reviewed. **Count** shows each skill over the reviewed postings with its interval; **Map** maps dashboards to CLO5; **Brief** prepares the brief and sends it to Dr Rahman.
4. **Decide.** Switch to **Dr Rahman** from the account menu. On **Home**, open the brief, write the reason and record **Adopt**. A decision on your own brief is labelled self-recorded and stays open until a second person confirms.
5. **Update.** Back as Dr Sobri, start a revision, change the outline within the hours, check it and adopt it. **History** shows both versions and the decision.
6. **Teach.** Save a session linked to CLO5 and download its teaching pack: editable slides with the evidence and no model text.
7. **Learn.** File the sample results under the term and the version taught; attainment per outcome shows n of N. The sample file's marks are synthetic, and the screen says so. No learning gain is claimed.

Then open **How it works** for the record of the afternoon by role, and **Evaluation** for the live checks and the suites.

## The local door

`node mcp/try.ts` starts the read-only MCP server over `mcp/sample-workspace.json` (the tour's end state) and asks it for CS310's evidence report through the official client. [docs/doors.md](docs/doors.md) has the Claude Desktop and Claude Code configuration. Remote access, OAuth and the other doors are planned.

## The pitch, the film and the print

- `deck/`: the eleven slides of the pitch, in the theme, with speaker notes; `npm run deck` checks every figure against [docs/claims.md](docs/claims.md) and exports the PDF and the PPTX.
- `video/`: the film's script, storyboard and style, the capture of the real app and the Remotion project for both cuts, rendered silent with captions until the team records the voices (`video/README.md` says how to render and check it).
- `docs/presentation/`: the brief (`lyceum-brief.pdf`), the A1 poster (`poster-a1.pdf`), the architecture diagram and the screenshots, all generated from the app and the tokens.

## Checks

`npm run prove:all` runs 185 checks in seventeen suites. After `npm run build`, `npm run smoke`, `smoke:integrity`, `smoke:workspace` and `smoke:curriculum` drive the built app in a headless browser, the first through the whole tour. [docs/as-built.md](docs/as-built.md) records every run with its date. These are software checks, not learning validation.

## What is real and what is not

- **Real:** requested board collection, exact-quotation validation, reviewed denominators, counts with intervals, decision records, checked revisions, teaching packs, results matched to versions, backups, the local read-only door. The saved sample is real Stripe postings retrieved on 9 October 2026, truncated and labelled as a saved sample.
- **Prototype:** the personas, the seeded courses and browser-local role switching. Sending a brief is an in-app local workflow, not a message to anyone.
- **Illustrative:** the sandbox's market figures and learner records; the sidebar labels the sandbox and its screens carry the notice. The sample results and survey files in `public/demo` hold synthetic marks and responses, and every record filed from them says so.
- **Planned:** the shared workspace and sign-in, the remote assistant doors, the LMS door, Learn's intervals, pooling and perception gap, the benchmark run, scheduled watching.
- **Never claimed:** representative labour-market counts, employability scores, learning gains or accreditation. No lecturer pilot has run. Formal approval stays in the university's process.

